{%- comment -%}
  ow-region-selector — the country/currency/language picker in the navbar and
  the mobile menu, backed by Shopify's real localization form.

  Accepts:
  - id_suffix: {String} unique per instance ('header', 'menu') — the selector
    appears more than once per page and form ids must not collide.
  - inline: {Boolean} true adds navbar-region--inline, the menu-modal variant.
  - country_filter: {String} optional comma-separated ISO codes ('HK, TW, GB').
    Blank shows every country Markets publishes. See THE FILTER IS A NARROWING
    below — it cannot add a country.

  Usage:
  {% render 'ow-region-selector', id_suffix: 'header', inline: false %}

  THE FILTER IS A NARROWING, NOT A LIST. localization.available_countries comes
  from the store's Markets settings; Liquid cannot invent a country that Markets
  does not publish, and shipping to one it does not cover would fail at
  checkout. So country_filter can only hide rows. A code in the filter that
  Markets does not publish is silently ignored, which is the safe direction.
  The same one-way rule governs languages: available_languages is whatever
  Settings > Languages publishes, and there is no setting here to extend it.

  The current pair always renders, filter or no filter. Hiding the market the
  shopper is actually in would leave the button showing a country absent from
  its own list.

  COUNTRY x LANGUAGE, ONE FORM. Each row sets country and language together,
  because Shopify's localization form takes country_code and locale_code in a
  single submit — offering them as one choice is what the form actually models.

  The cross-product is a genuine limitation, not a shortcut. Liquid exposes two
  FLAT lists — localization.available_countries and
  localization.available_languages ("the languages that are available on the
  store") — and no per-market mapping between them. There is no
  country.available_languages. So this pairs every surviving country with every
  published language, and it cannot know that a language is published for one
  market but not another. When that is wrong Shopify does not error: it falls
  back to the market's default language, so the row appears to do nothing. If
  the store ever publishes a language that is not global, narrow the countries
  with country_filter, or the pairing has to move to a metafield recording the
  mapping Liquid cannot see.

  Row count is countries x languages, so it grows fast: 5 countries and 3
  languages is 15 rows in a dropdown with no search. country_filter is the lever
  if that gets unwieldy.

  With one published language the language half disappears entirely and this
  renders exactly as the country-only version did — button 'HK ($)', rows
  'Hong Kong SAR (HKD $)'. Nothing to configure; the size check does it.

  The current pair renders first and is inert: no data-country, so the click
  handler in ow-chrome.js ignores it. That keeps the list a stable length
  instead of shuffling as the selection moves.

  The flag comes from ow-flags.css, a vendored slice of flag-icons whose class
  names are ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 lowercased — exactly country.iso_code | downcase,
  so 'TW' -> 'fi-tw' with no mapping table. A country whose flag has not been
  vendored yet renders an empty box; add it per the instructions in that file.

  Language is labelled with endonym_name — the language's name in itself, so a
  shopper who cannot read the CURRENT language still recognises their own. The
  compact button label uses the primary subtag only: 'zh-TW' shows as ZH,
  because 'TW ($) - ZH-TW' repeats the region twice in one label.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign current = localization.country -%}
{%- assign current_lang = localization.language -%}

{%- comment -%}
  One published language means there is no language choice to offer. Everything
  below keys off this rather than off available_languages.size directly, so the
  country-only path stays readable.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign multi_lang = false -%}
{%- if localization.available_languages.size > 1 -%}
  {%- assign multi_lang = true -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Resolve the filter, then count how many pairs survive it. The selector is
  only worth rendering when the shopper can actually switch to something, so the
  count — not available_countries.size — decides. Without this, a filter that
  narrows to a single market would render a dropdown whose only row is the inert
  current one.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign allowed = '' -%}
{%- if country_filter != blank -%}
  {%- assign allowed = country_filter | upcase | replace: ' ', '' | split: ',' -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- assign switchable = 0 -%}
{%- for country in localization.available_countries -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    `unless` rather than `if ... contains x == false`. Liquid has no operator
    precedence — it evaluates right to left and does not group — so a comparison
    chained onto `contains` inside an `and` does not mean what it reads like.
    For the same reason the current-pair test below is a nested `if`, never
    `a == b and c == d`.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if allowed != '' -%}
    {%- unless allowed contains country.iso_code -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endunless -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if multi_lang -%}
    {%- for language in localization.available_languages -%}
      {%- if country.iso_code == current.iso_code -%}
        {%- if language.iso_code == current_lang.iso_code -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- assign switchable = switchable | plus: 1 -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- if country.iso_code == current.iso_code -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- assign switchable = switchable | plus: 1 -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- if switchable > 0 -%}
  {%- assign current_lang_short = current_lang.iso_code | split: '-' | first | upcase -%}

  <div class="navbar-region{% if inline %} navbar-region--inline{% endif %}">
    <button class="navbar-lang" aria-haspopup="listbox" aria-expanded="false">
      <span class="fi fi-{{ current.iso_code | downcase }} navbar-region-flag"></span>
      <span class="navbar-region-label">{{ current.iso_code }} ({{ current.currency.symbol }}){% if multi_lang %} &middot; {{ current_lang_short }}{% endif %}</span>
      <img class="navbar-lang-chevron" src="{{ 'ow-chevron-lang.svg' | asset_url }}" alt="" width="16" height="16">
    </button>

    {%- comment -%}
      The id must be built before the tag. Writing
      `form 'localization', id: 'ow-localization-' | append: id_suffix`
      does not scope the filter to the id argument — Liquid re-reads the
      argument list and takes id_suffix as the form TYPE, failing with
      Invalid form type "header".
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign region_form_id = 'ow-localization-' | append: id_suffix -%}
    {%- form 'localization', id: region_form_id, class: 'navbar-region-form' -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="country_code" value="{{ current.iso_code }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="locale_code" value="{{ current_lang.iso_code }}">
      <ul class="navbar-region-list" role="listbox" aria-label="{{ 'localization.country_label' | t }}{% if multi_lang %} &amp; {{ 'localization.language_label' | t }}{% endif %}">
        <li class="navbar-region-option navbar-region-option--current"
            role="option"
            aria-selected="true"
            aria-disabled="true">
          <span class="fi fi-{{ current.iso_code | downcase }}"></span>
          {{ current.name }} ({{ current.currency.iso_code }} {{ current.currency.symbol }}){% if multi_lang %} &mdash; {{ current_lang.endonym_name }}{% endif %}
        </li>
        {%- for country in localization.available_countries -%}
          {%- if allowed != '' -%}
            {%- unless allowed contains country.iso_code -%}
              {%- continue -%}
            {%- endunless -%}
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if multi_lang -%}
            {%- for language in localization.available_languages -%}
              {%- if country.iso_code == current.iso_code -%}
                {%- if language.iso_code == current_lang.iso_code -%}
                  {%- continue -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endif -%}
              {%- assign lang_short = language.iso_code | split: '-' | first | upcase -%}
              {%- capture option_label -%}
                {{- country.iso_code }} ({{ country.currency.symbol }}) &middot; {{ lang_short -}}
              {%- endcapture -%}
              <li class="navbar-region-option"
                  role="option"
                  data-flag="fi-{{ country.iso_code | downcase }}"
                  data-label="{{ option_label | escape }}"
                  data-country="{{ country.iso_code }}"
                  data-locale="{{ language.iso_code }}"
                  aria-selected="false">
                <span class="fi fi-{{ country.iso_code | downcase }}"></span>
                {{ country.name }} ({{ country.currency.iso_code }} {{ country.currency.symbol }}) &mdash; {{ language.endonym_name }}
              </li>
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- else -%}
            {%- if country.iso_code == current.iso_code -%}
              {%- continue -%}
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- capture option_label -%}
              {{- country.iso_code }} ({{ country.currency.symbol }})
            {%- endcapture -%}
            <li class="navbar-region-option"
                role="option"
                data-flag="fi-{{ country.iso_code | downcase }}"
                data-label="{{ option_label | escape }}"
                data-country="{{ country.iso_code }}"
                aria-selected="false">
              <span class="fi fi-{{ country.iso_code | downcase }}"></span>
              {{ country.name }} ({{ country.currency.iso_code }} {{ country.currency.symbol }})
            </li>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- endform -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
